fact WorkItem(id: "WI-842", description: some(value: "Proposal 058 (WI-648) PHASE 3a — HARDEN the bracket-less provider readers (§4.9). This is phase 3b's PREREQUISITE, not its cleanup. AUDITED 2026-07-28: only ONE consumer can raise a use-site ambiguity — `resolve_inner` -> `pick_most_specific` (typing.rs:15030), which returns Ambiguous on a tie and is reachable only from `check_apply` (op-body calls and rule-body DOT-calls, exactly the sites that have a bracket). EVERY other consumer of provider facts selects by FIRST MATCH with NO ambiguity arm, and the code names today's LOAD-TIME refusal as its license — `witness_provision`'s own doc, verbatim (typing.rs:16114): \"Coherence (two witnesses for one application) is rejected at load … so first-match is the sole instance\". Phase 3b DELETES that premise, so these reads must be hardened FIRST or coexistence lands on silent first-match. THE RULE: a read that asks EXISTENCE stays BOOLEAN — two providers satisfy a constraint as well as one (`sort_provides`, the WI-300 `find_dictionary` and `[simp]` guards, typing.rs:31372: two providers are MORE true, the guard fires and hands the real choice to a selecting read). A read that SELECTS — returns one provider's op binding — goes LOUD ON THE SECOND CANDIDATE, never first-match: `provider_spec_view_bindings` (typing.rs:18192, returns at :18229; ~15 call sites including type conformance and WI-664's NonEq classifier; the \"first-provider-wins contract\" is named at load.rs:283), `witness_provision` / `witness_op_for_carrier` (typing.rs:16116, :16164), and the value-directed `or_else` chain (eval.rs:934) — which is what rule-body / constraint-body / quantifier-goal / `proof … by derivation` spec-op atoms fall to, since they are never typer-classified (`type_rule_bodies`, typing.rs:33543, recurses through a plain Apply without check_apply). Diagnostics in the spirit of WI-737's `Error[RelationFloundered]`: named, at the read. THE SEM-EQ INDEX IS THE SPECIAL CASE and refuses at LOAD instead: it is built by a bare `HashMap::insert` in `sort_ops.keys()` walk order (load.rs:4790-4845, mod.rs:3200), so under coexistence the winner would be NON-DETERMINISTIC PER LOAD, and no call site exists ANYWHERE to bracket — the dispatch fires from unification (resolver eq/neq builtin; interpreter `sem_eq_dispatch_target`, eval/builtins.rs:549). An index has no later site to complain from. INCLUDES WI-837 as an integral part: a WI-450 witness-supplied `eq` never keys that index (load.rs:4811-4813 reads `carrier_own_op .or_else(instance_fact_eq_target)`), so without it the new index check has NOTHING TO FIRE ON and the witness pair sails through both checks once 3b removes `AmbiguousWitness`. CONSEQUENCE TO STATE HONESTLY (§6): hardening the index means two PartialEq/Eq providers for one carrier remain a LOAD error — for that family tier 3's coexistence simply never applies, because semantic equality dispatches from unification where no selection can ever be written. ACCEPTANCE (§9 phase 3a, as amended): a two-PartialEq-provider program is a load error RAISED BY THE INDEX BUILD — assert the DIAGNOSTIC IDENTITY, not merely that the load fails, because `AmbiguousWitness` (typing.rs:15995) already refuses that exact program today and a failure-only assertion would pass with nothing implemented; the WITNESS-supplied pair (WI-837) is refused by the same check; a two-provider rule-body dispatch is a NAMED diagnostic, never first-match; existence guards (`sort_provides`) still fire. Full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: docs/proposals/058-modular-instances.md §4.9, §6, §9 phase 3a."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-837", "WI-838"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-29T21:47:50Z"))

